  "notes": "⛔ **The fingerprint the research calls \"a genuine Quik fingerprint\" is the loop length: 4 *or 5* bars.** The engine has no 5-bar loop, and the nearest honest thing it does have is `melody.phraseBars` reaching 4 with 2 and 1 under it plus the widest `chordDurationBeats` in this batch — an odd, non-power-of-two chord length is what a 5-bar loop sounds like to a bar counter. It is named here rather than silently dropped. The rest is the richest-documented entry in the section: the heaviest swing in the batch (0.65) with pronounced 16th ghost notes and live-feel hats over congas, timbales and tambourine; \"replayed-funk harmony\" where notes are strategically changed but the tempo is kept, so major-mode funk/disco sources sit over minor bass motion — authored as a chord set that keeps `III`, `VII` and a borrowed `V` against a bass in continuous 8ths. Bass is live and melodic, E1–G2, so `followRootsProb` 0.34 with an 18-semitone register and the densest bass cell shapes here. `high` is the research's own rating. ⚠ Two things the research asks for that the dataset cannot say: the talkbox (he worked with Roger Troutman from 1992) has no `timbreHint`, so the JD-800 lead is authored instead; and the tape saturation of the 4-track Tascam origins is a tone, not a note. ⚠ **The 1-bar phrase length was removed** (was [4,2,1], now [4,2]). A one-bar phrase looped four times carries less entropy than a four-bar phrase, and it is the countermelody it strangles hardest: a counter answers *inside* the melody’s phrase, so a one-bar shape leaves it nowhere to go. Every worst offender on `counter.rs` carried it. ⛔ The previous session recorded this exact mistake and it arrived again from the other direction — distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count.",
